---
import Layout from "@/components/Layout.astro";
import { propagation, meta } from "@/lib/load";
import { formatDate } from "@/lib/format";
import { withBase } from "@/lib/paths";
import { FIELD_SOURCE_LABEL, PROFILE_QUESTIONS } from "@/lib/profile-labels";
import type { FieldSource } from "@/lib/profile-labels";

const sources: FieldSource[] = ["standard", "policy", "ai-plan", "tracker"];

const corpusStart = meta.corpusStart ? formatDate(meta.corpusStart) : "November 2025";

const tierCounts: Record<"added" | "present-at-start" | "tied", number> = {
  added: 0,
  "present-at-start": 0,
  tied: 0,
};
for (const c of propagation.clusters) {
  const t = c.firstObserved?.tier;
  if (t) tierCounts[t] += 1;
}
---

<Layout
  title="Reading the data"
  description="How to read the change summaries, statement profiles, adoption charts, originality figures and reuse heat-map on this site — and what each of them does not claim."
>
  <div class="page reading prose stack">
    <h1>Reading the data</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Most Commonwealth agencies are required to publish a statement about how they
      use AI. This site watches those statements, keeps every version, and turns the
      changes into summaries, profiles, charts and colours. This page explains what
      each one means, and what it does not claim.
    </p>

    <h2 id="corpus">When the record starts</h2>
    <p>
      Continuous tracking opens on {corpusStart}, the week the Australian Public
      Service launched its AI Plan (the <a href={withBase("/about")}>About page</a>
      tells the origin story). But the statements themselves have been <a
        href="https://www.digital.gov.au/ai/ai-in-government-policy/standard-ai-transparency-statements"
        >required since February 2025</a
      >, so their first nine months, the period when agencies drew their wording
      from one another, fell before the tracker was watching. Everything that
      already existed enters the record together on day one, marked &ldquo;first
      tracked&rdquo; rather than &ldquo;published&rdquo;. We cannot know what those
      statements looked like before we first saw them. This is the single biggest
      limit on everything below.
    </p>

    <h2 id="reuse">Shared passages and the reuse heat-map</h2>
    <p>
      Agencies reuse one another&rsquo;s wording heavily. On each statement page,
      passages carry two different tints. An <em>amber</em> tint means the same
      passage appears in other agencies&rsquo; statements; the deeper the amber, the
      more widely it is shared:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>uncoloured</strong>: unique to that statement;</li>
      <li><strong>a few</strong>: shared with two or three other agencies;</li>
      <li><strong>several</strong>: shared with four to nine;</li>
      <li><strong>many</strong>: shared with ten to twenty-four;</li>
      <li><strong>most</strong>: shared with twenty-five or more.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      An amber passage also carries a small count &mdash; open it to see exactly which
      other agencies use the same words, each linked to its statement.
    </p>
    <p>
      A separate <em>cool grey</em> tint marks <em>template wording</em>: phrasing
      lifted straight from the Policy or the Digital Transformation Agency&rsquo;s <a
        href="https://www.digital.gov.au/ai/ai-in-government-policy/standard-ai-transparency-statements"
        >template</a
      >, the scaffolding most agencies start from. It gets its own colour, not
      a faint amber, because it is boilerplate rather than a passage we can trace to
      particular agencies &mdash; so it has no count to open.
    </p>
    <h3 id="first-observed">First observed in our corpus</h3>
    <p>
      For every shared passage we also record which tracked statement showed it
      earliest, and the order in which the others picked it up. Each passage falls into
      one of three tiers, by how much weight that ordering can bear:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>added during tracking</strong> ({tierCounts.added}): we watched the
        first agency add the passage well after the record opened, with others
        following later. The strongest signal.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>present at the start</strong> ({tierCounts["present-at-start"]}): the
        first agency already had the passage when tracking began, and others adopted it
        later. We can order the latecomers, but not that first agency&rsquo;s own source.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>tied</strong> ({tierCounts.tied}): several agencies share the earliest
        date, usually because they were all present on day one, so we make no claim
        about order.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Read this as <em>first observed by us</em>, never as proof of who wrote it
      first. A passage two agencies both carried on day one cannot be ordered at all,
      and even a clean ordering only says who we saw with it first. The real source
      could be an agency we don&rsquo;t track, an internal draft circulated off the
      web, or the DTA template itself.
    </p>

    <h2 id="originality">Originality scores</h2>
    <p>
      A statement&rsquo;s originality score is the share of its text, by length, that
      is <em>neither</em> shared with other agencies <em>nor</em> drawn from the
      template. A statement at 80% is mostly in its own words; one at 20% is mostly
      shared or templated language. The leaderboard and the agency grid colour each
      statement on this scale, from borrowed to bespoke.
    </p>
    <p>
      The DTA scores low by design: it publishes the template, so almost everything
      it says is, by definition, shared. We label it the template source rather than
      reading its low score as a lack of effort. Two other things push a score down
      without saying anything about effort. Shared text includes wording the Standard
      itself calls for (the document&rsquo;s title, its section headings, the OECD
      definition of an AI system that the policy adopts), so an agency that follows the
      Standard to the letter shares more than one that ignores it. And a statement two
      bodies publish jointly (the AASB and AUASB, for instance) is shared with itself.
      Read the score as &ldquo;how much of this text appears elsewhere&rdquo;, not as a
      grade.
    </p>

    <h2 id="changes">How changes are read</h2>
    <p>
      The scrape records every time a statement&rsquo;s text changes, and most of
      those changes are not the agency saying anything new: a rotating sidebar, a
      &ldquo;last reviewed 3 days ago&rdquo; counter ticking over, a page rebuilt with
      the same words. So every revision is classified from its diff. Mechanical
      rules settle the obvious cases (formatting only, links only, page chrome, a
      date stamp, passages reordered). Everything else is read by a language model
      (Claude) that is shown the diff and asked to sort it into one of five kinds and
      to write a one-sentence summary in plain English:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>scrape noise</strong>: nothing the agency wrote changed;</li>
      <li><strong>cosmetic</strong>: wording, typos, formatting, link targets, a date;</li>
      <li>
        <strong>expanded</strong>: the same substance at more length, such as a bullet
        grown into a paragraph or an example added to an existing claim;
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>restructured</strong>: reorganised or reworded, claims and commitments
        substantially the same;
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>substantive</strong>: a claim, commitment, fact or disclosure added,
        removed or altered.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Only the last three appear on the timeline and in the story on each statement page
      by default, and the site is precise about one distinction within them: a
      <strong>change of substance</strong> is exactly the substantive kind. Expanded and
      restructured revisions are shown alongside them &mdash; the statement now reads
      differently, and that is worth knowing &mdash; but they are never counted as
      changes of substance, because their definitions say the claims and commitments
      stayed the same. A revision nothing has yet read (rare: no rule matched and no
      model was available) is shown as <strong>unclassified</strong> rather than
      hidden, and counted as nothing until it is read. The model is told to report
      removals and additions alike, that an expansion which keeps the same substance is
      never substantive, and that text which vanished because the capture was cut short
      is scrape noise, not an edit. Every summary names the model that wrote it and is
      shown next to the raw diff, so you can check its reading against the words.
    </p>
    <p>
      One further guard sits outside the model: a capture we know to be broken is
      dropped before anything reads it, and one that suddenly lost most of its text
      is held off the site until confirmed. Dropping a capture can shift the date we
      observed a genuine change by a few weeks; we would rather that than a false
      change.
    </p>

    <h2 id="profiles">Statement profiles</h2>
    <p>
      Each revision of each statement is also read into a <em>profile</em>: a fixed
      set of questions with fixed vocabularies. Which DTA usage patterns and domains
      does it say are in use? Does it address public-facing AI, and does it commit to a
      human intermediary? Is an accountable official named? A Chief AI Officer, in
      place, planned, or not mentioned? A use-case register? Mandatory training? How
      often does it promise to be reviewed, and when does it say it was last updated?
      What does it explicitly promise not to do?
    </p>
    <h3 id="schema">Where the questions come from</h3>
    <p>
      Three instruments sit behind these statements: the policy that mandates them,
      the Standard that sets their minimum content, and the APS AI Plan (the <a
        href={withBase("/whos-who")}>who&rsquo;s who</a> page sets out how they fit
      together). Wherever one of them asks a question, the profile asks that
      question; where none does, the question is ours. The usage patterns and
      domains are the DTA&rsquo;s classification system verbatim. Nothing here is a
      requirement unless its source says so: the Chief AI Officer, in particular,
      comes from the APS AI Plan, not the policy.
    </p>
    <dl class="sources">
      {
        sources.map((source) => (
          <div>
            <dt>
              {FIELD_SOURCE_LABEL[source].href ? (
                <a href={FIELD_SOURCE_LABEL[source].href}>{FIELD_SOURCE_LABEL[source].long}</a>
              ) : (
                FIELD_SOURCE_LABEL[source].long
              )}
            </dt>
            <dd>
              <ul>
                {PROFILE_QUESTIONS.filter((q) => q.source === source).map((q) => (
                  <li>{q.label}</li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
    <p>
      Because the vocabularies are closed, two agencies&rsquo; profiles compare
      directly, and two revisions of one agency&rsquo;s profile can be diffed: that is
      where the &ldquo;commitment dropped&rdquo; and &ldquo;Chief AI Officer: planned
      &rarr; in place&rdquo; markers come from. A bullet point expanded into a paragraph
      about the same thing produces no profile change at all. The profile records only
      what the statement itself says, never what is typical, and it records job
      titles, never people&rsquo;s names.
    </p>
    <p>
      The reading is a model&rsquo;s, and it can be wrong: a commitment paraphrased
      two ways across revisions may read as dropped and re-added; a role described
      vaguely may land in the wrong box. To limit the drift, each revision is read
      with the previous revision&rsquo;s profile as its baseline, and changes are
      reported only on revisions whose diff was judged worth reading. Every
      profile sits beside the statement it came from, and every change beside its
      diff, so both can be checked.
    </p>

    <h2 id="adoption">Adoption over time</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={withBase("/policy")}>policy in practice</a> page counts, for each
      month since tracking began, how many tracked statements&rsquo; profiles report each
      item (a Chief AI Officer, a register, mandatory training, a reference to version
      2.0) and shows that as a share of the statements tracked that month. Three
      cautions. Early months have small denominators: only {meta.counts.statements}{" "}
      statements are tracked now, and fewer were on day one. Agencies joined in waves,
      so a month-on-month move can be new entrants rather than new text. And a
      statement that mentions a register is not proof the register exists; the charts
      show what agencies <em>say</em>, which is what a transparency statement is for.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two verdicts on that page rest on a statement&rsquo;s own dates.
      &ldquo;Updated since policy 2.0&rdquo; is true when the statement&rsquo;s
      self-declared last-updated date, or a change we observed ourselves, falls on or
      after 15 December 2025, and false when we have been watching since before then
      and have seen neither; where we cannot tell, we say so. &ldquo;Overdue&rdquo;
      is simpler: the Standard requires review at least yearly, so a self-declared
      date more than a year old is the agency&rsquo;s own admission, and a statement
      with no date is neither current nor overdue. Both verdicts move with the
      calendar, so both are dated.
    </p>

    <h2 id="more">The fine print</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={withBase("/about")}>About page</a> covers how all of this is
      computed: how spurious scrape churn is collapsed out, the exact passage-matching
      rules, and which model does the reading. It also sets out the limits of the
      scraping itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      One limit is worth keeping in mind. Because we read each statement straight
      from a live web page, what we capture is sometimes the page rather than the
      statement, and the classifier&rsquo;s job is to tell the two apart. It is good at
      it, not perfect. Follow the source link on any statement to check the original.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .reading {
    max-width: 46rem;
  }

  .reading h3 {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-4);
  }

  .sources {
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .sources dt {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .sources dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .sources ul {
    margin: var(--space-1) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }
</style>
